WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.400
More than

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.235
six feet of snow falling in parts

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.570
of Southern California.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.005
Hurricane force winds

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.439
whipping up blinding

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:07.741
dust storms in Texas

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:08.908
and powerful tornadoes

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.044
ripping through Oklahoma. And Kansas.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:11.778
Take a look at this.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.680
This is a scene out of Norman,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.982
Oklahoma, just south of Oklahoma City

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.283
where a tornado touched down.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.019
Police and firefighters are searching

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.553
through the wreckage this morning.

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.355
They say at least 12 people are injured.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.024
No reported deaths so far.

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:24.791
Listen to this woman

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:25.725
who was inside her home

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.095
with her family when the tornado struck

00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:31.865
Before I can even blink,

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.466
I could hear the wind coming.

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:34.467
All of a sudden,

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.735
all the back windows

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.470
where the kids bedrooms are.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.671
I can hear them

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:41.107
just crashing, busting out.

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:43.309
And I got up

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.512
and then the wind just threw me back

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:46.980
and I'm screaming.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.281
It was like a blizzard

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:49.249
inside the house

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:51.451
with all the debris flying.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:53.887
And I was screaming for my kids,

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.421
you know,

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.588
because they were in their bedrooms.

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:58.391
I didn't know

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:01.194
they were hurt or anything.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:02.195
Terrified mother.

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:02.729
Ed Lavandera

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.097
joins us this morning on the ground

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.432
in Norman, Oklahoma.

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:07.434
Ed, it looks terrible behind you.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.701
What happened

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.272
Well, this storm system ripped

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:15.008
across this state in breathtaking speed,

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.042
but it has left

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:17.143
haunting images

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:20.013
like these massive pieces of plywood

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.148
left in trees

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.485
A massive storm system

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:27.320
barreled through the Central Plains

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.021
Sunday night

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.022
to traditionally

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:30.924
the southern plains and Tornado Alley,

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:31.658
if you will.

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.726
It doesn't really start to come alive

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:34.627
with these tornadoes

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:36.162
and these severe weather outbreaks

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:37.864
until maybe March,

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.165
especially April and May.

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.233
That's the peak

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:41.968
tornado, severe season here

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.002
in the southern plans.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:43.636
But, you know,

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:46.039
so this is quite an early wakeup call.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:47.273
Multiple tornadoes

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.908
touched down in the region as well.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.343
In Norman, Oklahoma,

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.011
a tornado caused downed power

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.880
lines and road closures.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:54.781
This is terrible

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:55.982
to have this tornado

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:57.450
going through Norman like this.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.519
It's just past 48th and Lindsay

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:00.720
moving up to the northeast.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.588
And we're going to try to get

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:02.822
back on I-40 and get out

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:03.690
ahead of it again.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.225
We're going as fast as we can.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:06.526
That is not good

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:07.393
right there on the West Side

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:08.328
at Thunderbird, guys.

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:09.863
Oh, it's getting even stronger.

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.898
The storm ripped roofs off homes

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:13.466
and damaged cars.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.168
A lot of real strong wind.

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:16.803
I was standing out in the garage.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:17.737
My wife went to

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.173
the neighbor's to get in a shelter.

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:22.609
Oh, a wooden debris

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:25.778
started flying and hitting things.

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.481
So I jumped in the back seat of a car

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:29.115
in the garage.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:29.883
Real quick,

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.251
two tornadoes were reported

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:32.785
in Kansas on Sunday,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.787
leaving homes in the area destroyed.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.590
Winds in the triple digits were felt

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.826
the highest in Memphis, Texas,

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:43.563
where winds hit a staggering 114

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:44.931
miles per hour,

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.499
the equivalent of a Category

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.902
three hurricane in Lubbock, Texas.

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.837
A dust storm rolled through,

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.772
leaving visibility in the area

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.007
to less than a mile.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.010
And in Albuquerque, New Mexico,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.279
winds topped over 70 miles per hour,

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:00.480
leaving overturned

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.682
trees and businesses damaged.

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:05.218
I'm in shock, you know, but

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.586
because we love our restaurant,

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.121
we we love our work,

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.557
this amount of damage with this wind.

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:12.091
I haven't seen anything like this

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:12.592
for the 20

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.360
plus years I've been living here.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.763
This storm system started in California,

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:18.898
where parts of Southern California

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.400
saw a rare storm

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.101
that dumped massive amounts

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:23.903
of snow in the area.

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:24.571
Huge snow

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:26.372
totals were seen throughout the region,

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.840
including Mountain High,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.575
which recorded

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:31.945
93 inches of snow through Sunday morning.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:33.580
Rain was dumped

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.181
on other parts of California,

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:36.216
causing flooding

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:39.285
and leaving cars stranded

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.888
California firefighters were seen

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.523
rescuing this driver

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:44.791
by helicopter

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.993
as floodwaters continue to rise.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.862
The storm left the ground so eroded

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.431
that this RV

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:52.999
fell into the Valencia River.

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:53.800
I'm just kind of afraid

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.768
or not to evacuate if it gets any worse.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:03:59.305
And probably if

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:01.174
there is a couple of silver linings

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:02.375
to all of this.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:03.610
The worst kind of storm damage

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.811
like you're seeing behind me

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:06.879
has been very isolated into the areas

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:09.282
where some of these tornadoes fell down

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:10.383
rather quickly.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:12.151
So that is good news

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:12.719
in terms of

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.120
just it's not widespread

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.355
damage across the state,

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:17.624
but also with this wind event

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:18.491
and the intense winds

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:20.660
that we've seen over the last 24 hours

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.427
here in the state,

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:22.428
there was a great concern

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.697
of widespread power outages.

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:25.365
We're about

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.800
30,000 customers in the state of Oklahoma

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.569
without power this morning.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:30.370
And we've noticed that

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.638
that number has been dropping

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:33.940
rather quickly in these overnight hours.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.907
So hopefully that's something

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.342
that gets under control

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.477
rather quickly for residents

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:38.511
here in Oklahoma as well.

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:39.646
I really hope so.

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:40.213
Ed Lavendera,

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:41.447
thank you to you and your team

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:42.782
for being there.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:43.883
Let's get to our meteorologist now.

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:44.784
Chad Myers said

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:46.619
this is some very strange weather.

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:47.453
A friend in L.A.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.022
out of her home for three days.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:50.089
Her power was out,

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:51.958
downed trees in her neighborhood.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:53.226
This is in Los Angeles,

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:54.927
right?

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:55.962
Especially in the L.A.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:57.830
mountains, too, with all of that snow,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.832
some spots over 60 inches of snow.

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:01.267
It's the same energy,

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.268
the same low pressure

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:04.270
that moved through Oklahoma yesterday

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.140
with a very serious situation here.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.809
And as Ed alluded to, over 100

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.611
mile per hour wind gusts.

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:12.445
These storms, though,

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.580
we're moving at 80 miles per hour.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:15.782
Those are storms you can't chase

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:16.816
you can't get out of the way.

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.384
When you hear your warning

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:18.951
for storms

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:19.352
like this,

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:20.653
you must get in the basement

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.322
or someplace safe.

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:23.623
Here's 10:00 last night.

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:24.290
Here's Oklahoma

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.625
City and Norman especially

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:27.293
now look where this storm is now.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.696
You could hardly drive that quickly

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:30.797
to where it is now.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:32.532
To Little Rock, Arkansas.

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:34.100
The severe weather will be to the east.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.569
Winds are still gusting about 55 miles

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:37.103
per hour.

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:37.770
The severe weather

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.038
centered over Indianapolis

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:40.973
and also over Columbus, Ohio.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:43.176
But look at this for 11:00 tonight.

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.378
It starts to snow in New York City

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:46.412
and then it even begins

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.814
to snow in Boston.

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:48.881
I haven't really said

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:51.017
Boston and snow in the same sentence

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:51.751
all year.

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:52.752
So this could be a one

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.387
to five inch snowfall across

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:55.488
parts of the Catskills,

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:56.222
probably closer

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:57.557
to two to four for the city

00:05:57.557 --> 00:06:00.293
and then two to four for Boston itself.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.828
We'll still watch this,

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:03.963
but a slick morning commute coming up

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:05.631
tomorrow morning for sure.

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:06.799
Don, when was the last time you said

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.201
New York City and snow as well

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:09.302
in New York? I know.

